WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.160
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.099
Most of the techniques we've seen in previous
fluid papers run the simulation inside the

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:12.299
entire volume of the fluids.

00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:17.500
These traditional techniques scale poorly
with the size of our simulation. But wait,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.310
as we haven't talked about scaling before,
what does this scaling thing really mean?

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:27.160
Favorable scaling means that if we have a
bigger simulation, we don't have to wait longer

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.519
for it. Our scaling is fairly normal if we
have a simulation twice as big and we need

00:00:32.519 --> 00:00:38.079
to wait about twice as much. Poor scaling
can give us extraordinarily bad deals, such

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:43.179
as waiting ten or more times as much for a
simulation that is only twice as big.

00:00:43.179 --> 00:00:48.969
Fortunately, a new class of algorithms is
slowly emerging that try to focus more resources

00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:53.870
on computing what happens near the surface
of the liquid, and try to get away with as

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.120
little as possible inside of the volume.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.530
This piece of work shows that most of the
time, we can get away with not doing computations

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.640
inside the volume of the fluid, but only on
the surface.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.310
This surface-only technique scales extremely
well compared to traditional techniques that

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:15.500
simulate the entire volume. If a piece of
fluid were an apple, we'd only have to eat

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.830
the peel, and not the whole apple. It's a
lot less chewing, right? As a result, the

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:25.610
chewing, or the computation, if you will,
typically takes seconds per image instead

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:26.280
of minutes.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.320
A previous technique on narrow band fluid
simulations computed the important physical

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:37.590
properties near the surface, but in this case,
we compute not near the surface, but only

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:43.450
on the surface. The difference sounds subtle,
but it makes a completely different mathematical

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:44.020
background.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:49.170
To make such a technique work, we have to
make simplifications to the problem. For instance,

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.830
one of the simplifications is to make the
fluids incompressible. This means that the

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:56.909
density of the fluid is not allowed to change.

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:02.140
The resulting technique supports simulating
a variety of cases such as dripping water,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:06.920
droplet and crown splashes. fluid chains and
sheet flapping.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:12.370
I was spellbound by the mathematics written
in the paper that is both crystal clear and

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:19.310
beautiful in its flamboyancy. This one is
such a spectacular paper. It is so good, I

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.870
had it on my tablet and couldn't wait to get
on the train so I could finally read it.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:29.590
The main limitation of the technique is that
it is not that useful if we have a large surface

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:35.069
to volume ratio, simply because the peel is
still a large amount compared to the volume

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:40.069
of our apple. We need it the other way around
for this technique to be useful, which is

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:41.620
true in many cases.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.700
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

